# 2026-09-22 (Task 7 fix round 1, review IMPORTANT 2): 1.5.0-alpha -> 1.5.1-alpha.
#   DERIVED TEXT CHANGES, so this is a BUMP and not one of the "genuinely
#   cannot affect what is derived" re-pins above.
#
#   WHAT CHANGED: src\analysis\DRagLint.Analysis.PurityStage.pas (hashed whole,
#   `::*`) stopped composing the witness as 'touches ' + AFacts.Touches.
#   symbol_facts.touches is the TWO-FIELD wire string 'resources|transactions'
#   whose separator is ALWAYS present when anything was found, so one-sided
#   values are stored as 'file system|' or '|starts, commits' -- measured on
#   this repo's own index: 308 rows 'file system|', 13 rows
#   '|starts, commits, rolls back', 1 row with both sides. Concatenating printed
#   the separator. The new TouchesWitness splits on '|' and omits an empty side,
#   the same way DRagLint.Doc.Regions already renders the fact, and labels the
#   transaction half.
#
#   WHY IT REACHED A USER AND NOT JUST A COLUMN: the new query-name-with-effect
#   rule (spec section 14, same task) quotes effect_witness verbatim in its
#   message, so the first corpus run printed 'FindHgRoot answers a question and
#   also has an effect: touches file system|'.
#
#   SCOPE, stated precisely so the bill is judged rather than assumed: VERDICTS
#   ARE UNCHANGED. effect_free and effect_summary are computed from the same
#   flags as before -- ApplyFacts still calls AddFlag(efGlobal, ...) on exactly
#   the same condition. Only the WITNESS TEXT moves, and only on routines whose
#   touches fact is non-empty.
#
#   WHY BILL A RE-RESOLVE FOR DISPLAY TEXT: TPurityStage recomputes only while
#   PurityNeedsRun is True (some body row still has effect_free IS NULL). A
#   database already filled under 1.5.0-alpha would keep the old witness text
#   FOREVER -- there is no other trigger -- and this repo's own self-index has
#   already been resolved under 1.5.0-alpha. Re-pinning within 1.5.0 would be
#   exactly the silent staleness this file's header warns about in capitals.
#   Remedy: index --all --resolve-only.
#
#   WHY 1.5.1 AND NOT 1.6.0: 1.6.0 is reserved by the engine backlog for C2.3 +
#   the IsStub unification (see the T6-R2 entry above). Consuming it for a
#   witness-text fix would make the real bump lie about what it contains.
#
#   DEVIATION FLAGGED TO THE CONTROLLER: Task 7's plan ruling 11 said
#   DRAGLINT_RESOLVER_VERSION must not move in this task. That ruling was written
#   when Task 7 touched no resolve surface; the fix round then assigned a change
#   to PurityStage.pas, which this manifest hashes whole. Both cannot hold. The
#   bump is taken because this baseline's own discipline makes it mandatory for a
#   derived-data change; it is one commit and two lines to revert.
#
#   Superseded: 1.5.0-alpha|5ec79c8c501a536a2f202355a5148f3dd87cae9d8c524f5d0fe609f50edb9509

# 2026-09-22 (whole-branch fix wave, review IMPORTANT 1; controller ruling T-FW1):
#   hash re-pinned WITHIN 1.5.1-alpha -- the "genuinely cannot affect what is
#   derived" case, same discipline as the 2026-09-06 (C1b), 2026-09-07,
#   2026-09-19 and T6-R2 comment-only entries above.
#
#   WHAT MOVED THE HASH: a `dl:ok` review marker was RELOCATED onto the class
#   declaration line inside src\analysis\DRagLint.Analysis.PurityStage.pas,
#   which is on the resolve surface as
#   `src/analysis/DRagLint.Analysis.PurityStage.pas::*` and is therefore hashed
#   WHOLE -- comment text included, so a marker living in a comment is inside
#   the hashed span. The branch's marker had been placed ABOVE the declaration
#   and carried @e3b0, the SHA-256 prefix of the EMPTY STRING, i.e. a hash never
#   computed from the annotated text; the engine matches a marker only within
#   the finding's own StartLine..EndLine, so god-class and high-response both
#   REPORTED and review-marker-unused reported on the marker line. `drag-lint
#   allow ... --fix-rule god-class/high-response --apply` rewrote it on the
#   finding's own line with real hashes (@13c7) and the dead line above was
#   deleted; the human reason text moved into the marker's own Reason field.
#
#   PROVEN comment-only, not asserted. `git diff -U0 4a23e034 e19f7e14 --
#   src\analysis\DRagLint.Analysis.PurityStage.pas`, classifying every changed
#   line by the code text to the LEFT of its `//`:
#
#       raw diff lines                        : -2 / +1
#       pure-comment lines removed            :  1  (the dead marker line)
#       lines whose CODE TEXT is unchanged    :  1 removed / 1 added, identical
#                                                (`  TPurityRun = class`, with a
#                                                 trailing `//` comment appended)
#       NON-comment (code-text) lines changed :  0
#
#   Zero. That zero is the load-bearing number: a `dl:ok` marker is a LINT
#   annotation that lives in a comment. It cannot change which call_edges,
#   type_ancestors, type_helpers or unit_uses rows the resolve pass derives, and
#   it cannot change a single symbol_facts.effect_free, effect_summary or
#   effect_witness value. No routine body, signature or literal moved, so NO
#   INDEX OWES A RE-RESOLVE.
#
#   ATTRIBUTION MEASURED, NOT INFERRED. The same fix wave also normalised a
#   stray double space inside SYMBOL_FACTS_BASE_COLS in
#   src\storage\DRagLint.Storage.SQLite.pas. That file is on the surface per
#   named ROUTINE, and the const is inside none of them -- checked by running
#   this guard twice with one edit reverted at a time:
#
#       SQLite edit reverted, PurityStage fixed -> guard exit 1 (FAIL)
#       PurityStage reverted, SQLite fixed      -> guard exit 0 (PASS)
#
#   So the whole-file `::*` entry is the SOLE cause of the move; the store edit
#   does not touch the hashed surface at all.
#
#   CONTRAST WITH TODAY'S OTHER CALL, recorded so a future reader sees both side
#   by side. The entry immediately above (Task 7 fix round 1) BUMPED 1.5.0 ->
#   1.5.1 for a change inside this same file, and that was right: it altered the
#   WITNESS TEXT written into symbol_facts.effect_witness, which is a DERIVED
#   row, and a database already filled under 1.5.0 would have kept the old text
#   forever because TPurityStage recomputes only while PurityNeedsRun is True.
#   A re-pin there would have carried a false justification. Here nothing
#   derived changes, so a bump would bill every database a re-resolve for a
#   comment -- and, worse, would consume a version number for a lint annotation.
#   The distinction is DERIVED-ROW CHANGE vs COMMENT TEXT, not which file moved.
#
#   1.6.0 remains reserved by the engine backlog for C2.3 + the IsStub
#   unification (see the T6-R2 entry above); nothing here touches that.
#
#   Same warning as every entry above: the failure mode of getting this wrong is
#   SILENT.
#
#   Superseded: 1.5.1-alpha|92cfb2a2e060dcf3261caaa542c8b1d74ff2919a952b76c6383f97f547551bef
# 2026-09-23 (enum-value-ref binding, Task 5): 1.5.1-alpha -> 1.6.0-alpha. This
#   is a BUMP, not one of the "genuinely cannot affect what is derived" re-pins
#   above -- a NEW CLASS OF DERIVED ROW starts being written.
#
#   WHAT CHANGED, by file and routine:
#     tests\resolver-surface.txt -- three entries ADDED to the manifest, which
#       is itself part of the hashed identity (path::routine is hashed with the
#       body): src/storage/DRagLint.Storage.SQLite.pas::ResolveEnumValueRefs
#       (the new store stream), ::GetEnumValueSymbols and
#       ::GetUnitLevelValueDecls (the two bulk readers that supply the
#       candidate set and the R3(c) shadow set). ResolveEnumValueRefs matches
#       the guard's drift vocabulary (`resolve`), so it had to be classified or
#       the guard fails on drift -- it did fail that way after Task 4. The two
#       readers match no vocabulary term and are listed by this manifest's own
#       CLOSURE RULE: what they return decides what is written. Checked against
#       the exclusion list before adding -- none of resolvelog, resolvesecs,
#       resolvefileidtolerant or resolvetypeat shadows any of the three names.
#     src\index\DRagLint.Index.CallResolver.pas -- already hashed WHOLE
#       (`::*`); Task 3 added the enum candidate/shadow maps,
#       ResolveEnumValueRead (R1 visibility, R2 uniqueness, R3 shadowing, R4
#       unit-level code, rule-0 duplicate collapse) and rung 3c for the
#       qualified `TEnum.value` / `Unit.value` shape.
#     src\storage\DRagLint.Storage.SQLite.pas -- Task 4 added the second stream
#       inside ResolveCallTargets, the NULL-over-its-own-universe UPDATE that
#       makes a SCOPED re-resolve able to RETRACT a binding, and the ValueOnly
#       write branch that calls FQSetRefSymbol and writes no edge row.
#     src\core\DRagLint.Core.ForwardStub.pas -- hashed WHOLE, so the one
#       comment line naming the reserved version (1.6.0 -> 1.7.0, see below)
#       moves this hash too. It is NOT the reason for the bump; the derived
#       rows are. Called out because the T-FW1 entry directly above bumped
#       nothing for a change to exactly this kind of line.
#     src\core\DRagLint.Core.Model.pas -- the constant and its <para> history.
#       The three Model.pas routines on the surface (GenericArity,
#       SplitGenericName, StripGenericSegments) are untouched; only the
#       implementation section is hashed, so the const and its doc comment are
#       outside the hashed span by construction.
#
#   WHY THIS IS A BUMP AND NOT A RE-PIN. The calls stage now writes
#   refs.symbol_id on two ref shapes that were NULL on every index ever built:
#   a bare `read` ref naming an enum value, and a `member-access` ref qualified
#   by the enum type or by the declaring unit. That is a new class of DERIVED
#   ROW, not new text in an existing one. A database resolved under 1.5.1-alpha
#   has NONE of them, and -- this is the load-bearing half -- it would never
#   acquire them on its own: the calls stage runs WHOLE-DB only on a
#   resolver-fingerprint mismatch, and otherwise runs SCOPED over the files
#   that changed. Files that never change would keep NULL forever. The stamp is
#   the only trigger there is.
#
#   SCOPE -- what does NOT change, stated so the bill can be judged rather than
#   assumed. No call_edges row and no member_accesses row is written for an
#   enum value (CanBeCallTarget is untouched); CallSiteRefKindSql, the
#   COMPLEMENT universe the unresolved-call readers take, is not widened
#   (register item E1); no table, no column, no SCHEMA_VERSION move (23
#   unchanged); no extractor change (DRAGLINT_EXTRACTOR_VERSION 1.17.0-alpha
#   unchanged -- the refs already exist and are indexed, this is a resolve-time
#   binding). Purity verdicts are unchanged: TPurityStage reads `read` refs by
#   Kind/NameText against the routine's locals and never consults SymbolId for
#   them, and its edges come from call_edges and member_accesses, to which
#   nothing is added. MEASURED, not asserted -- Task 4's M4 A/B on this repo's
#   self-index, the same parses re-resolved by the pre-change engine and by the
#   new one (`index --project ... --resolve-only`):
#
#       run                 edges  accesses  proven  not_proven  not_computed
#       M4_A (old engine)   11137     10366     187        2757             0
#       M4_B (new engine)   11137     10366     187        2757             0
#
#   Every column equal, and both runs reported the same
#   `21493 edge(s) from 65719 call-site ref(s)`. The enum-value invariant was
#   checked directly on the same database: 0 call_edges rows and 0
#   member_accesses rows whose target/member symbol is an enum_value.
#
#   REMEDY AND COST. A RE-RESOLVE, never a re-parse: `index --all
#   --resolve-only`. Measured 2026-09-22: ~6 min for all project sections; up to
#   ~73 min per platform library -- Win32 calls 3641.4 s + purity 726.8 s =
#   4368.2 s = 72.8 min, Win64 3500.6 s + 635.3 s = 4135.9 s = 68.9 min. Neither
#   figure is 74; the headline is the larger of the two, not rounded past it.
#   The enum stream itself adds one indexed name-IN scan
#   over `refs` (EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN on the self-index:
#   `SEARCH refs USING INDEX idx_refs_name_nocase`) plus an in-memory lookup
#   per candidate -- seconds on a project DB.
#
#   WHY 1.6.0 AND NOT 1.5.2. This file's own discipline, applied to itself: a
#   PATCH is for a change to DERIVED TEXT inside rows that already exist (1.5.0
#   -> 1.5.1 was the purity witness STRING), and a MINOR is for a new class of
#   derived row. This is the second. Shipping a new binding kind under a patch
#   number would mislead exactly the reader this file is written for -- someone
#   deciding whether their database owes a re-resolve.
#   1.6.0 WAS RESERVED for C2.3 + the IsStub unification, by the T6-R2, Task 7
#   and T-FW1 entries above. The owner ruled on 2026-09-23 that the reservation
#   was a SEQUENCING note, not a semantic claim about the number, and that the
#   enum binding -- which lands first -- takes 1.6.0-alpha. C2.3 + IsStub move
#   to 1.7.0-alpha. Those three entries above are append-only history and are
#   left exactly as written; the forward references a future reader will
#   actually consult were edited in FOUR places, and all four now say 1.7.0:
#     1. src\core\DRagLint.Core.Model.pas -- the 1.5.0 -> 1.5.1 <para>'s
#        "A PATCH bump, not 1.6.0" sentence.
#     2. src\core\DRagLint.Core.ForwardStub.pas -- the unit's header comment,
#        "deferred to DRAGLINT_RESOLVER_VERSION 1.7.0".
#     3. CHANGELOG.md -- the Unreleased `### Known` bullet on
#        ResolveTypeNameToClass.IsStub.
#     4. docs\BACKLOG-SESSION-100-TRIAGE.md -- the three C2.3 rows (the
#        decisions table, the sequencing list, the open-questions table). That
#        file is gitignored (`docs/BACKLOG-*`), so it is edited in the working
#        checkout and is not part of this commit.
#   Whoever writes C2.3's plan will reach one of those four; none of them still
#   says 1.6.0.
#
#   CONTRAST WITH THE ENTRY ABOVE, recorded so the two adjacent calls can be
#   read side by side. T-FW1 RE-PINNED within 1.5.1 for a `dl:ok` marker
#   relocated inside a whole-file-hashed unit: comment text, 0 code lines, so
#   no derived row anywhere could differ and a bump would have billed every
#   database a re-resolve for an annotation. This entry BUMPS, for the opposite
#   reason: rows that did not exist begin to exist. The distinction that
#   decides it is the one T-FW1 itself states -- DERIVED-ROW CHANGE vs COMMENT
#   TEXT, not which file moved. Note that this change ALSO edits a comment line
#   in the same whole-hashed ForwardStub.pas that T-FW1's sibling edit sat in;
#   on its own that would have been a re-pin, and it is not what is being
#   billed here.
#
#   Same warning as every entry above: the failure mode of getting this wrong
#   is SILENT. A database that owes a re-resolve and does not get one does not
#   error, does not warn and does not return an empty result -- it answers
#   confidently with fewer rows than the truth.
#
#   Superseded: 1.5.1-alpha|8d1034e88ce8debb8a6e1d8e11c67c6905ff8c0fa65d50f11893eb4c6ffb4c16

# 2026-09-23: 1.6.0-alpha -> 1.7.0-alpha (parenless-call binding, defect D1).
#   A THIRD calls-stage stream, ResolveParenlessCallRefs ->
#   TCallResolver.ResolveParenlessRead, writes a call_edges row (and
#   refs.symbol_id for a certain edge) for a `read` ref that is a PARENLESS CALL
#   -- `Assert(NextId > 0)`, `N := NextId`, a bare `Tick` inside its class. Rows
#   that did not exist begin to exist, so this BUMPS. 1,715 candidate reads on
#   ORM3 CLIENT at 1.6.0 (59 routine names). No parse change: the remedy is
#   `index --all --resolve-only`, never a re-parse.
#   The manifest also gained GetUnitLevelRoutines and GetUnitScopeEdges, readers
#   the Option 4 unit rung already depended on and the manifest never listed;
#   their hash rides on this bump.
#   The reservation for C2.3 + the IsStub unification moves from 1.7.0 to
#   1.8.0-alpha.
#
#   The ForwardStub.pas header comment (whole-file surface) was updated in the same
#   change to name 1.8.0 as the C2.3 + IsStub reservation; comment text only.
#
#   Superseded: 1.6.0-alpha|95080b86976b780299f60f1f3e4c5fec372170db4b3fb980b23feca790aadec6
